{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And you shall serve HaShem.</b> Perhaps it follows from what was stated above (Exodus 23:24), for when one breaks the power of idolatrous worship and demolishes its strongholds, through this he serves HaShem, and he draws goodness upon himself, and HaShem blesses his bread." "And His saying <b>and your waters</b> — meaning, that from the waters are born weakening and the products of illnesses and evil humors; therefore He promised that He would bless his waters, so that they would cause the growth of the human body in perfect health, as is known to those who understand the assessment of waters, for after living waters a person will wander to settle there. And a great promise the Creator, blessed be He, promised in this, and therefore He juxtaposed to the blessing of the waters ״וְלֹא תִהְיֶה מְשַׁכֵּלָה״ etc. Go forth and learn that which they of blessed memory expounded (Berakhot 59b) concerning the waters of the Euphrates." "<b>And I will remove</b> etc. HaShem marked out the components of blessing in three things: the first — that the person be healthy to delight in the goodness of HaShem, and that is His saying <b>And I will remove</b> etc., for without this all food nauseates the soul. The second — He will increase his children, and that is His saying <b>there shall be no miscarriage</b> etc. The third — that he complete his days in length of days and years of life, and that is His saying <b>the number of your days</b> etc. In this too HaShem showed the superabundance of the blessing, for without these three things there is no recognition of blessing, for if the foodstuffs were meager and bereft of health and the years few — whatever HaShem gives suffices for their needs, and so it turns out He blessed his bread and his waters, yet there is nothing great in this promise except through the mediation of these three things."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:33:06.636867383Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}